Vendored dependency: PractRand, by Chris Doty-Humphrey

  Upstream:      https://sourceforge.net/projects/pracrand/
  Version:       0.96
  Archive:       https://sourceforge.net/projects/pracrand/files/PractRand_0.96.zip/download
  SHA-256:       e4caf7fda98b2c597bbda3b576753cf5a0f6047aab837c82be370ab798a672e1
  Released:      2025-12-26
  Imported:      2026-10-24
  License:       public domain, with the CC0 1.0 dedication as fallback
                 (src/practrand/LICENSE, doc/license.txt upstream)
  Local patches: applied in this order, from tools/patches/
                 0001-no-stdio.patch
                 0002-cran-warnings.patch
                 0003-portability.patch

src/practrand/ is tools/vendor/derive's output: upstream's include/ and src/
trees without RNGs/other/, and doc/license.txt as LICENSE, with line endings
normalised from CRLF to LF, then exactly the patches above applied.
tools/vendor/verify re-derives the tree from the pinned archive and fails on
any other difference.

Only the library is imported. tools/ (RNG_test, TestManager.h and the other
command-line programs), doc/ and VisualC_net/ are left out; TestManager.h
and RNG_test.cpp are the reference for the package's own driver. The
"NotRecommended" generators in include/PractRand/RNGs/other/ and
src/RNGs/other/ are left out as well: upstream's licence notes that some
were copied from web pages, forum posts and papers, with no licence stated,
and nothing else in the library includes or links them. The other 21
translation units are compiled: rand.cpp needs the seeding code, the
recommended RNGs and non_uniform.cpp.

Upstream is the SourceForge project. Version 0.96 (2025-12-26) is newer than
every fork found on GitHub on 2026-10-23 (MartyMacGyver/PractRand,
rurban/PractRand, tylov-fork/PractRand, all at 0.94 or the 2019 pre-0.95
snapshot, none updated since 2022), so the release is vendored rather than a
fork. SourceForge publishes no checksum; the SHA-256 above was computed at
import and is re-checked by tools/vendor/verify (trust on first use).

0001-no-stdio.patch removes everything the library could write to stdout or
stderr, and every exit(), which R CMD check forbids in compiled code even
when unreachable. Every std::printf call, nearly all in calibration and
debugging code, goes to PractRand::Internals::no_printf(), a no-op declared
in config.h. issue_error() throws std::runtime_error instead of printing and
calling exit(1) when no handler is hooked, and the three exit() calls in
tests_core.cpp and tests_other.cpp call issue_error() instead. The patch is
mechanical; tools/vendor/make-no-stdio-patch regenerates it from the
pristine tree.

0002-cran-warnings.patch makes the library compile without a significant
warning under CRAN's gcc and clang flags (-Wall -pedantic), as R CMD check
judges them, and without the diagnostic-suppressing pragmas R CMD check
warns about. Measured 2026-10-24 with GCC 15 and Apple clang 17: about 150
significant warnings each before, none after. The edits change no
behaviour, with one exception:

  - unused locals and typedef asserts are marked used with (void)name; or
    (void)sizeof(name); on the declaring line, never deleted;
  - dead static functions and tables are marked [[gnu::unused]];
  - constructor initialiser lists are written in declaration order, which
    is the order C++ initialises members in anyway (-Wreorder);
  - the unsigned __int128 convenience wrappers are compiled out with
    PRACTRAND_NO_INT128 in config.h; nothing in the library calls them;
  - reads of the repeated blocks before `data` (as32[-1], as64[-n]) go
    through a pointer cast instead of a negative array index, the same
    access without -Warray-bounds;
  - two variables that are only unset on a path that ends in issue_error()
    are initialised to 0, and mrsf32 writes ~Uint32(0) instead of ~0ull;
  - the exception: tests_other.cpp read `ored` uninitialised in a
    function that returns (ored >> BITS) == 0; it now starts at 0. That
    is an upstream bug, and the result was undefined before.

The template shifts by BITS_PER_BLOCK in NearSeq that the pragmas hid
(-Wshift-count-overflow) still warn; they are not significant to R CMD
check. 0002 is hand-made: on an update, apply it to the new tree, fix what
no longer applies, and regenerate it with diff -ruN against the tree with
0001 alone (tools/vendor/derive with only 0001 listed).

0003-portability.patch, also hand-made, covers what 0002 missed because it
was measured as C++17 on x86_64 against R 4.5.2's list of significant
warnings. R-devel's list adds C++20 deprecations, R now compiles packages
as C++20 by default, and CRAN's macOS builds are arm64:

  - platform_specifics.cpp includes <x86intrin.h> and calls __rdtsc() for
    any GNU compiler; both are now limited to x86, and the fallback, which
    returned a clock object rather than a count, returns the count;
  - arithmetic between enumerators of different anonymous enums, or between
    an enumerator and a double, is deprecated in C++20; on each such line
    the enumerators get a unary +, which promotes them to their integer
    type without changing the value;
  - three more reads of the blocks before `data` go through a pointer cast,
    as in 0002, for GCC's -Warray-bounds at C++20.

Measured 2026-10-24 against R-devel's src/library/tools/R/check.R, with
GCC 15 (C++17 and C++20) and Apple clang 17 (C++17; C++20 for x86_64 and
arm64): no significant warnings. Regenerate 0003 against the tree with 0001
and 0002 applied.
